<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malay Peninsula</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malay Peninsula is the long peninsula of South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> extending south from mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> between the Andaman Sea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. Its control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> made it strategically valuable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>. In the early modern period the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> competed there for control of trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula.jpg" alt="Malay Peninsula" width="287" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dino Eri · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Semenanjung_Malaka.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
